  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 1159 AM MST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nQuiet weather will continue on Friday, with highs once again in the \n50s-60s, with dry and mild conditions.\n\nStarting on Saturday, the upper ridge over us will push east, with \nsome energy moving through the Four Corners and into our forecast \narea. Though the day will start off mild and dry, moisture will push \ninto the area from the west by late afternoon. This will kick off \nsnow showers over the mountains first, with rain showers moving over \nthe lower terrain by Saturday evening. \n\nLatest data shows snow levels remaining around 8-9 kft, lowering \ntowards 7 kft into early Sunday. Latest snow totals have risen a bit \ncompared to previous estimates. The San Juans are now expected to \nreceive widespread 3-5 inch accumulations, though localized amounts \nmay rise as high as 8 inches. Models remain in decent agreement on \nthe path of this quick-moving low, which has increased forecast \nconfidence a bit, though totals are still expected to change as the \nsystem draws closer and is better resolved. So, no highlights needed \nquite yet. The rest of the central and southeast mountains will get \nmuch lighter snow, with most of the peaks getting less than 2 \ninches, though portions of the Sangres may see up to 3. Though, \ndepending how quickly the system passes us by, it's possible some of \nthe guidance may be overdoing totals slightly.\n\nAfter a brief lull in activity Monday, with slightly warmer and \ndrier conditions, a new system is expected to move in.\n\nLong-range guidance continues to suggest a broad trough moving over \nthe western CONUS, with moisture pushing into our CWA sometime \naround late Tuesday. However, the disagreement between model \nsolutions remains substantial, so details have yet to be narrowed \ndown. Generally cooler and unsettled weather expected around the \nmiddle of next week.",
